## Monospace X-Height Inflation Mechanics

Technical documentation routinely mixes inline code (`<code>`) into body copy.
Sans-serif typefaces (e.g. Inter, SF Pro) and monospace code faces (e.g. JetBrains Mono, SF Mono) carry fundamentally divergent font metrics:
To maximize legibility in code editors, monospace typefaces open up their letter counters, and **their x-height tends to run a little taller than sans-serif type at the same size**. Measured from the macOS system font files, as a fraction of the font size: PingFang SC 0.517, SF Pro 0.508, SF Mono 0.526, Menlo 0.547. The monospace faces are only about 1.7%–7.7% taller.

## Optical Alignment: Scaling Inline Code to 0.9em

If inline code inherits `font-size: 1em`:
- Monospace tokens stick out awkwardly like unharmonized patches.
- Inflated height distorts uniform line-height rhythms.

TuffEx's standard solution lives in its style entry: `<code>` inside `.tx-prose` that is not in a `<pre>`, and any element with `tx-inline-code`, scales to 0.9em.

:::TuffCodeBlock{lang="scss"}
---
code: |
  // packages/tuffex/packages/components/style/index.scss
  .tx-prose :not(pre) > code,
  .tx-inline-code {
    padding: 0.15em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background-color: var(--tx-fill-color-light, #f5f7fa);
    font-family: var(--tx-font-family-mono);
    // Scale to 0.9em to hold back the monospace face's taller x-height
    font-size: 0.9em;
    vertical-align: baseline;
  }
---
:::

The components that render inline code themselves (TxStreamText, TxMarkdownView, TxMarkdownEditor, TxChainOfThought) scale it to 0.9em as well; TxStreamMarkdown and the body of these docs use 0.875em.

0.9em is an approximation. The size that lines the two x-heights up is the body x-height divided by the code x-height: measured in Chrome on macOS, these docs set body text at 0.508em and code, which falls back to Menlo, at 0.547em, a ratio of 1.077, so an exact match takes 0.929em and 0.9em sits 3.1% short. Across the pairs of faces above, the exact match falls between 0.93em and 0.98em. The figure below measures both x-heights in your browser.

